;;; `owed-seats.ein` with **Ann renamed Zed**, and nothing else — the twin that
;;; makes its wrong answer an order dependence rather than only a wrong count.
;;; Regression `owed-seats`, as a twin.
;;;
;;; The walk's lattice answered k = 3 here too, but its third model was
;;; `{(seats Bob C1), (seats Bob C2), (seats Zed C1)}` — mapped back, Bob on
;;; both chairs and Ann on C1, where the original file's third model put **Ann**
;;; on both chairs. Renaming a constant changed which superset the name-sorted
;;; prefix join built, so it changed the model set. A model set that moves
;;; under a renaming is wrong under every reading of Q-M1e2.1.
;;;
;;; A bare `ein solve` answers the same seven states, renamed, in both files.
(import std.algebra :symbols (total-owed surjective-owed))
(relation is-a   T T :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation seats  Guest Chair :why "{?1} sits on {?2}")
(is-a Zed Guest) (is-a Bob Guest)
(is-a C1 Chair)  (is-a C2 Chair)
(total-owed      seats is-a)
(surjective-owed seats is-a)
(query :goal (seats ?g ?c))
